,title,link,text,language,family
0,Church Slavonic - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_Slavonic,"Church Slavonic is a Slavic language belonging to the South-Slavic linguistic sub-branch of Balto-Slavic languages, in the Indo-European family. It is used, both historically and in the modern era, as a liturgical language by various Eastern Christian denominations.","Slavonic, Church",Indo-European
1,Music - Old Church Slavic and Church Slavic: Primary and ...,https://guides.loc.gov/old-church-slavonic-and-church-slavic/music,"Apr 29, 2026 · This research guide is intended to be a starting point for research on Church Slavic and Old Church Slavic sources found at the Library of Congress, including print materials, digital resources and microfilm.","Slavonic, Church",Indo-European
2,Old Church Slavonic Chant Powerful Slavic Orthodox Choir ...Liturgy in Church Slavonic - YouTubeAECMC All-European Church Music Committee | Conferences ...Ancient Slavonic Chants | Smithsonian Folkways RecordingsThe Church Slavonic E-Tutor,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ljbiTnQpnaM,"Nov 26, 2025 · Perfect for meditation, prayer, studying, or anyone seeking powerful ancient Christian music. Featuring authentic Old Church Slavonic lyrics, cathedral reverb, and the emotional depth of... Oct 13, 2024 · This recording transforms the original 2108 score by adapting the text into Church Slavonic, the richly sonorous language used by much of the Eastern Orthodox church today. The Committee provides a series of internet lectures of well-known specialists in the Church Music field, consisting of four blocs: History of Church Singing; Church Slavonic; Typicon; and Choral Methods. The liturgical chants in this recording—originally released by Monitor Records in 1994—come from the monastic tradition of medieval Bulgaria and are sung in Old Church Slavonic, the earliest recorded language of the Slavic peoples. Our rich hymnography and music are imbued with its rhythm and meter. While English translations and musical arrangements have made huge strides in conforming to traditional Orthodox style, rarely do they approach Slavonic's spiritual majesty and splendor.","Slavonic, Church",Indo-European
